{"bill":{"id":"119-sjres-71","congress":119,"billType":"sjres","billNumber":"71","title":"A joint resolution terminating the national emergency declared with respect to energy.","introducedDate":"2025-07-31","latestActionDate":"2025-10-08","latestActionText":"Failed of passage in Senate by Yea-Nay Vote. 47 - 51. Record Vote Number: 554.","policyArea":"Emergency Management","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Alternative and renewable resources","Building construction","Coal","Energy storage, supplies, demand","Licensing and registrations","Mining","Oil and gas"],"sponsorBioguideId":"K000384","cosponsorBioguideIds":["H001046","W000802","B001288","S000033","R000122","V000128","M000133","S001181"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution terminates the national emergency relating to energy declared by the President on January 20, 2025, in Executive Order 14156. The executive order states that the supply of and infrastructure for energy in the United States is insufficient to meet the country's needs. It defines energy as crude oil, natural gas, lease condensates, natural gas liquids, refined petroleum products, uranium, coal, biofuels, geothermal heat, the kinetic movement of flowing water, and critical minerals. The executive order directs the heads of executive departments and agencies to use available emergency and other authorities to take certain actions to address this topic, including approving development of domestic energy resources, expediting the completion of authorized energy infrastructure (particularly in the Northeast, West Coast, and Alaska), and pursuing the use of emergency permitting provisions under certain environmental regulations. The executive order also directs the Department of Defense to conduct an assessment of its ability to acquire and transport energy resources (particularly in the Northeast and West Coast), and invokes emergency military construction authority to address any vulnerabilities identified in the assessment.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/71","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/sjres/BILLSTATUS-119sjres71.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:25.490Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-sjres-71","plainSummary":"This joint resolution terminates the national emergency relating to energy declared by the President on January 20, 2025, in Executive Order 14156. The executive order states that the supply of and infrastructure for energy in the United States is insufficient to meet the country's needs. It defines energy as crude oil…\n\n(No LLM key configured, so this is the official summary text rather than a plain-English paraphrase. Set LLM_PROVIDER in .env for readable summaries.)","industries":[{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Keyword match on \"renewable\" in the bill text. No LLM key configured, so this was not read semantically."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Keyword match on \"Coal\" in the bill text. No LLM key configured, so this was not read semantically."},{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Keyword match on \"Mining\" in the bill text. No LLM key configured, so this was not read semantically."},{"industry":"defense","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Keyword match on \"Defense\" in the bill text. No LLM key configured, so this was not read semantically."}],"method":"keyword-fallback","model":null,"inputHash":"17e73919558b2ba267a0418f","classifiedAt":"2026-07-29T01:28:01.572Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would end the national emergency relating to energy declared by the President on January 20, 2025, in Executive Order 14156.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys fuel or heats a home. People who live near a mine, and people who work in one.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what fuel costs. Nothing has change